<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of Trent</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1545">1545</a>–<a href="/years/1563">1563</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Tridentine Council</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> was the ecumenical council of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> that met at Trent in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in three periods between 1545 and 1563. In response to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> it reaffirmed Catholic doctrine on scripture and tradition, justification and the seven sacraments, and ordered reforms including seminaries to train priests and the residence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> in their dioceses. Its decrees shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> until the twentieth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/council-of-trent.jpg" alt="Council of Trent" width="320" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laurom · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Concilio_Trento_Museo_Buonconsiglio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> issued his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninety-five-theses">95 Theses</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> on 31 October 1517. In 1520 he appealed to the German princes for a council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, free of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, and German opinion came to see a council as the best way to reconcile the differences. It took a generation to meet. Popes feared reviving the schism over conciliarism, the Lutherans demanded the exclusion of the papacy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> were rivals, and the Turkish danger weighed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Paul III summoned a council to Mantua for 23 May 1537, but war between France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> prevented it, and it was postponed indefinitely in 1539.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The council met for twenty-five sessions between 13 December 1545 and 4 December 1563, in three periods: 1545–1549, 1551–1552 and 1562–1563. It opened at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> with only about 30 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. Julius III reopened it on 1 May 1551, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-elector-of-saxony">Maurice, Elector of Saxony</a>, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> and marched into Tirol in April 1552. Protestants had been invited and given safe conduct, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Melanchthon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brenz">Johannes Brenz</a> set out for Trent, but they were refused a vote and the victory of Maurice ended their cooperation.</p><p>Pius IV reconvened it from 18 January 1562. The French monarchy boycotted the council until a delegation led by Charles de Guise, Cardinal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, arrived in November 1562. The decrees were signed in 1563 by 255 members, two-thirds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> being Italians. No pope attended in person; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legatus">legates</a> represented them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The council affirmed that scripture and tradition are both authoritative, confirmed the traditional canon including the deuterocanonical books, endorsed the Latin Vulgate and reaffirmed the seven sacraments. On justification it declared that grace is offered through human cooperation, rejecting the Protestant doctrine, and it reaffirmed purgatory, indulgences and the veneration of saints, while forbidding the sale of indulgences. Pius IV ratified the decrees on 26 January 1564. The Tridentine Creed followed in 1565, and Pius V issued the Roman Catechism and revised the Breviary and Missal in 1566, 1568 and 1570, which led to the Tridentine Mass. Italy, Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> accepted the decrees, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> only recognised their doctrinal parts. More than three hundred years passed before the next ecumenical council, in 1869.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Council of Trent” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was the chief power of the Catholic Counter-Reformation: Ignatius of Loyola founded the Society of Jesus, Teresa of Ávila and John of the Cross reformed the Carmelites, and Spanish theologians shaped the <b>Council of Trent</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Reformers Martin Luther and John Calvin wrote works that defined Protestant theology, and the <b>Council of Trent</b>, from 1545 to 1563, defined Catholic teaching in response.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Council of Trent</b>, opened by Pope Paul III, was tasked with institutional reform, addressing corrupt bishops and priests, the sale of indulgences and other financial abuses.</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>The Catholic Church, at the <b>Council of Trent</b> between 1545 and 1563, reaffirmed the teachings the reformers rejected, among them the seven sacraments, the sacrifice of the Mass and the authority of tradition alongside scripture, while also reforming many of the abuses they had attacked.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Council of Trent</b> later suppressed it as a sequence of the Mass, together with hundreds of other sequences.</blockquote><a href="/songs/stabat-mater">Stabat Mater</a></li><li><blockquote>Before the <b>Council of Trent</b> (1545–1563) many feasts had sequences of their own, and some sixteen different sequences were in use for Easter alone.</blockquote><a href="/songs/victimae-paschali-laudes">Victimae paschali laudes</a></li></ul></section>
